in this lesson you're gonna learn how to  draw an angle in radians in standard  position or sometimes refer to as  sketching an angle in standard position  but we're gonna do it in radians which  might be new to you you might be used to  degrees now all of a sudden they're  teachers saying we're working with  radians the main thing to remember  though is that PI radians that's the  same thing as 180 degrees so whatever  you see PI you can replace that with 180  degrees now when we draw an angle in  standard position we have what we call  the initial ray that's where it's kind  of like a spinner like imagine if you  were like spinning this it's going to  either rotate counterclockwise or it's  gonna rotate clockwise now if it rotates  counterclockwise we refer to that as a  positive angle if it rotates clockwise  that's actually a negative angle it's a  little counterintuitive now the main  thing is you want that initial ray along  the positive x axis and then wherever  the ray or the spinner ends up that's  what we call the terminal ray that's  where it terminates your stops and then  we just draw an arrow showing the  direction whether we were going  counterclockwise or clockwise now some  angles and radians that are good  remember they come up a lot are like PI  over six that's equal to 30 degrees  because remember pi is 180 180 divided  by six is thirty another one that's good  to remember is PI over four that's 45  degrees again 180 divided by four PI  over three that's sixty degrees because  180 divided by three PI over two that's  90 degrees and we can have multiples of  these but the main thing here is is that  you want to think about like for example  PI over six what this is it's like  taking PI which is like 180 degrees and  you're dividing up into six portions  like one two three four five six right  like six pizza slices and so if we were  going let's say for example two PI over  six that means you're going one two  you'd end up right there and so this is  our initial ray this is our terminal  rate and that's how we would draw it let  me show you some examples I think it'll  get clearer as we go through this say  for example number one 25 PI over six  how would you sketch that  the first thing you want to do is want  to draw our initial ray okay along that  positive x-axis and then what I like to  do especially when it's an improper  fraction is turn it into a mixed number  so six goes into 25 how many times four  with one left over so that's really like  4 and 1/6 pi well we know that pi is a  hundred and eighty degrees so that's  gonna be like 1 PI 2 pi 3 pi C I'm going  half a revolution 4 pi  and now we're gonna go one six of Pi so  one sixth of this half revolution which  is going to be right about there now  it's helpful to remember that PI over  six is thirty so you know what 30  degrees is you can go just a little bit  you know past and you've got it now we  have our arrow to show the direction of  rotation and that will help as well  let's look at number two now so number  two we have negative seven PI over three  so first thing I would do is convert  this into a mixed number three goes into  seven twice with one left over so this  is really like negative 2 and 1/3 pi  negative means we're going clockwise  right let's start over here at the  positive x-axis that's our initial rate  we're going 1 pi 2 pi and now we're  going that would be 3 pi but we're just  going 1/3 of the way we know that pi  over 3 C 1/3 pi is the same as PI over  you know that's a 60-degree angle so  we're going like about 60 degrees more  and that's our terminal race so we  basically went one revolution plus  another 60 degrees  okay last example see if you can get  this one 19 PI over 4 4 goes into 19 how  many times 4 times with 3 left over so  that's really like 4 and 3/4 pi this is  positive so remember when it's positive  we're going counterclockwise so we're  gonna go 1 PI 2 pi 3 PI 4 PI and now 3/4  of pi well another PI would put us all  over here at 180 degrees but we're going  3 quarters of the way which is going to  be right about there and you've got it  if you want to learn more about radians  I did a more comprehensive  that's a video about what a Radian is  and how to work with radians and  coterminal angles and reference angles  and more follow me over to that video  right there  and we'll go over that there
 

